{
  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 12,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.224,
            0.088,
            0.589,
            0.173
          ],
          "content": "**Example 6.6:** A sports car of mass 1500 kg is moving towards the east and its velocity-time graph is shown in Fig. 6.21. Calculate the force acting on the car during"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.236,
            0.176,
            0.352,
            0.193
          ],
          "content": "(i) 0 s to 5 s"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.23,
            0.196,
            0.361,
            0.213
          ],
          "content": "(ii) 5 s to 10 s"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.226,
            0.217,
            0.371,
            0.234
          ],
          "content": "(iii) 10 s to 15 s"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.226,
            0.238,
            0.31,
            0.254
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Answer:"
        },
        {
          "type": "chart",
          "bbox": [
            0.597,
            0.072,
            0.902,
            0.242
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.658,
                0.244,
                0.906,
                0.274
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 6.21: Velocity-time graph for a sports car"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_12_chart_5.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.225,
            0.258,
            0.406,
            0.276
          ],
          "content": "(i) During 0 s to 5 s:"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.224,
            0.279,
            0.889,
            0.33
          ],
          "content": "As the velocity-time graph is a straight line inclined to the time axis, it indicates that the sports car is moving with a constant acceleration during this time interval with u = 0 m s<sup>–1</sup>, v = 10 m $\\mathbb { S } ^ { - 1 }$ and t = 5 s."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.254,
            0.332,
            0.868,
            0.35
          ],
          "content": "Using the kinematic equation $\\upsilon = u + a t ,$ we can find the acceleration,"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.255,
            0.351,
            0.49,
            0.368
          ],
          "content": "10 m s<sup>–1</sup> = 0 m s<sup>–1</sup> + (a × 5 s)"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.256,
            0.372,
            0.352,
            0.389
          ],
          "content": "a = 2 m s<sup>–2</sup>"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.224,
            0.393,
            0.887,
            0.428
          ],
          "content": "Now, using the Newton’s second law of the motion F = ma, we can find the force acting on the sports car as,"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.255,
            0.43,
            0.732,
            0.449
          ],
          "content": "F = 1500 kg × 2 m s<sup>–2</sup> = 3000 N acting towards the east."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.225,
            0.451,
            0.422,
            0.468
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "(ii) During 5 s to 10 s:"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.224,
            0.47,
            0.889,
            0.521
          ],
          "content": "As the velocity-time graph is a straight line parallel to the time axis, it indicates that the sports car is moving with a constant velocity. Hence, no force is acting on the sports car."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.225,
            0.523,
            0.443,
            0.541
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "(iii) During 10 s to 15 s:"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.223,
            0.543,
            0.889,
            0.611
          ],
          "content": "As the velocity-time graph is a straight line inclined to the time axis, it indicates that the sports car is moving with a constant acceleration in this time interval with u = 10 m s<sup>–1</sup>, v = 0 m s<sup>–1</sup> and t = 5 s. Using the kinematic equation v = u + at  , we can find the acceleration,"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.255,
            0.612,
            0.493,
            0.629
          ],
          "content": "0 m s<sup>–1</sup> = 10 m s<sup>–1</sup> + (a × 5 s)"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.256,
            0.633,
            0.364,
            0.65
          ],
          "content": "a = – 2 m s<sup>–2</sup>"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.224,
            0.654,
            0.888,
            0.689
          ],
          "content": "Now, using the Newton’s second law of the motion F = ma  , we can find the force acting on the sports car as,"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.255,
            0.691,
            0.557,
            0.709
          ],
          "content": "F = 1500 kg × (– 2 m s<sup>–2</sup>) = – 3000 N"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.224,
            0.712,
            0.888,
            0.747
          ],
          "content": "The negative sign shows that the force is acting in a direction opposite to the direction of motion, that is towards the west."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.253,
            0.752,
            0.312,
            0.783
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_12_image_23.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.319,
            0.759,
            0.508,
            0.778
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Pause and Ponder"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.253,
            0.79,
            0.857,
            0.823
          ],
          "content": "6. A toy car of mass 100 g is moving with a constant velocity of 0.5 m s<sup>–1</sup>. What is the net force acting on the toy car?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.254,
            0.825,
            0.859,
            0.873
          ],
          "content": "7. Two children of different masses are sitting on identical swings. To impart identical initial acceleration, for which child would you require to apply a larger force? Explain why."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.253,
            0.875,
            0.858,
            0.908
          ],
          "content": "8. How are glass items packed for transportation using a bubble wrap or hay protected from damage?"
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.074,
            0.942,
            0.114,
            0.96
          ],
          "content": "106"
        },
